{"causes": ["The ongoing Roman expansion into Hispania Citerior led to persistent conflicts with indigenous tribes like the Numantines, who fiercely resisted Roman subjugation.", "The inherent difficulty of campaigning in unfamiliar and rugged terrain, combined with the Numantines' guerrilla tactics, posed significant challenges to conventional Roman military strategy.", "Internal political rivalries within the Roman Senate weakened oversight and coordination of military campaigns, contributing to the autonomy and potential misjudgment of field commanders like Mancinus."], "effects": ["The humiliation of Mancinus's treaty and subsequent extradition intensified Roman resolve to conquer Numantia, culminating in its destruction by Scipio Aemilianus in 133 BCE.", "The legal and ethical debate surrounding Mancinus's actions highlighted the complex relationship between a commander's authority, senatorial approval, and the sanctity of treaties in Roman law.", "Mancinus's disgrace contributed to a growing public discourse on military accountability and the moral integrity of Roman leadership, influencing later political reforms."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "gaius-hostilius-mancinus", "sourceName": "Gaius Hostilius Mancinus", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "numantine-war", "targetName": "Numantine War", "context": "Gaius Hostilius Mancinus served as a Roman consul during the Numantine War, leading a significant military campaign against the Celtiberians in 137 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "gaius-hostilius-mancinus", "sourceName": "Gaius Hostilius Mancinus", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "treaty-of-mancinus", "targetName": "Treaty of Mancinus", "context": "Gaius Hostilius Mancinus negotiated and signed the humiliating Treaty of Mancinus with the Numantines in 137 BCE to save his trapped army."}, {"sourceSlug": "roman-senate", "sourceName": "Roman Senate", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "gaius-hostilius-mancinus", "targetName": "Gaius Hostilius Mancinus", "context": "The Roman Senate's rejection of Mancinus's treaty and subsequent order for his extradition profoundly influenced his political fate and public image."}, {"sourceSlug": "gaius-hostilius-mancinus", "sourceName": "Gaius Hostilius Mancinus", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "hispania-citerior", "targetName": "Hispania Citerior", "context": "Mancinus's military campaign and the infamous Battle of Numantia occurred within the Roman province of Hispania Citerior in 137 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "publius-cornelius-scipio-aemilianus-africanus", "sourceName": "Publius Cornelius Scipio Aemilianus Africanus", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "gaius-hostilius-mancinus", "targetName": "Gaius Hostilius Mancinus", "context": "Publius Cornelius Scipio Aemilianus Africanus succeeded Gaius Hostilius Mancinus in command against Numantia, ultimately conquering the city in 133 BCE."}], "places": [{"name": "Numantia, Spain", "role": "Site of major defeat"}, {"name": "Rome, Italy", "role": "Political center, Senate's judgment"}, {"name": "Hispania Citerior, Spain", "role": "Province of campaign"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Gaius Hostilius Mancinus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "gaius-hostilius-mancinus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q949544", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gaius_Hostilius_Mancinus"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Gaius Hostilius Mancinus's defeat and treaty at Numantia profoundly impacted Roman military and political policy, directly leading to Scipio Aemilianus's decisive campaign and the ultimate destruction of Numantia. His public disgrace also sparked crucial debates on senatorial authority and military accountability within the Roman Republic.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-27T01:35:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40432858017", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Gaius Hostilius Mancinus was a politician and general of the Roman Republic. He is mostly known for his defeat against the Numantines as consul in 137 BC and the humiliating treaty he signed afterwards in order to save his army.", "newValue": "Gaius Hostilius Mancinus, consul of the Roman Republic in 137 BCE, became an enduring symbol of military disgrace and political controversy during the late Republic. His catastrophic defeat by the Celtiberian Numantines in Hispania Citerior and the subsequent treaty he negotiated to spare his trapped legions, triggered a profound constitutional and moral crisis in Rome. This episode illuminated the complex interplay between senatorial authority, popular will, and military necessity during a period of intense imperial expansion and internal strife, contributing to the broader narrative of Rome's struggles in Spain.

Commanding two consular armies in Hispania Citerior, Mancinus faced a formidable adversary in the resilient Numantine people, who had repeatedly defied Roman authority. After a series of tactical blunders and a demoralizing ambush, Mancinus found his 20,000-strong army encircled and facing annihilation. To prevent a complete massacre, he agreed to a humiliating peace treaty with the Numantines, recognizing their independence in exchange for the lives of his soldiers. This pact, negotiated without senatorial authorization, was a desperate measure that saved his troops but brought immense shame upon Rome, echoing earlier defeats like the Caudine Forks.

Upon his return to Rome, Mancinus's treaty was vehemently rejected by the Senate, who viewed it as a national dishonor. The Senate, influenced by figures like Publius Cornelius Scipio Aemilianus Africanus, voted to extradite Mancinus back to Numantia, stripped naked and bound, as a symbolic gesture of nullifying the treaty and absolving Rome of its terms. Although the Numantines refused to accept him, viewing the Roman act as a breach of divine law, Mancinus endured public humiliation and political exile, eventually being re-admitted to the Senate only after a prolonged legal battle, a testament to the contentious nature of his actions and their aftermath."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-27T01:35:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40432858017", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "150 BCE, http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/843a3e9a529dbf27c7135f2717a6f22b"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "150 BCE, http://www.wikidata.org/.well-known/genid/843a3e9a529dbf27c7135f2717a6f22b"}], "newValue": ["The ongoing Roman expansion into Hispania Citerior led to persistent conflicts with indigenous tribes like the Numantines, who fiercely resisted Roman subjugation.", "The inherent difficulty of campaigning in unfamiliar and rugged terrain, combined with the Numantines' guerrilla tactics, posed significant challenges to conventional Roman military strategy.", "Internal political rivalries within the Roman Senate weakened oversight and coordination of military campaigns, contributing to the autonomy and potential misjudgment of field commanders like Mancinus."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-27T01:35:17Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#40432858017", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type"